// SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-3.0-or-later
//
// An un-discharged nullable appended to a non-null collection FIELD warns and the store
// proceeds — it does not panic, and it does not go quiet.
//
// `(N-Store)` rejects storing `e:τ?` into a non-null `τ` slot, and its severity is decided by
// REPRESENTABILITY, not by taste: a hard error only where the null sentinel collides with a
// real value of τ — the narrow widths, and nothing else — and a WARNING everywhere the null is
// representable-and-distinct, where the store compiles and runs. A collection is stored
// out-of-band (`nullref`), so it is on the warning side. `d.c = s` one operator over already
// warns and proceeds, which is the reading this file holds `+=` to.
//
// The append path asked nobody (loft#1210). `s_type` is what the routes below it read to
// choose between concat, single-element push and the keyed fill, and an `Optional` matched
// none of them — so the value fell to the push, which writes whatever it is handed as ONE
// element of the element type. A `vector<integer>?` was written where an `integer` belongs:
// the interpreter panicked into a read-only const store (`compile.rs::compile (CONST_STORE
// init)`) and `--native` emitted `set_int(…, v)` with a `DbRef` for `v` — E0308, so the
// program could not be built at all. Neither symptom named the statement that caused it; the
// interpreter reported a position inside `default/05_coroutine.loft`, which the program does
// not use. A KEYED destination took the same value quietly instead, which is the half that
// says the hole was wider than the crash.
//
// The cure peels the source where `convert` already peels it for `=`, so both operators reach
// the same reading of the same value, and the `(N-Store)` warning is what tells the author.
//
// Controls, and each is load-bearing:
//   * the DENSE source is the reference route — same statement, no `?` — and it must stay
//     correct AND silent, so a cell where it warns means the gate widened past nullability;
//   * both DISCHARGED spellings (`s?`, `s ?? []`) are what the warning tells the reader to
//     write, so they must answer what the undischarged one now answers and warn about nothing
//     — a guard whose cure is itself diagnosed would be advertising a dead end;
//   * an ACTUALLY-null source separates "the store proceeds" from "the store proceeds with
//     something"; appending an absent vector appends nothing, and the destination keeps
//     exactly what it held;
//   * a nullable ELEMENT (`integer?`) rather than a nullable COLLECTION, because the element
//     and the collection reach the routes by different arms of the same gate.
//
// The vector LOCAL is the fifth control and it is NOT here: it is refused, by a different rule
// (@PLAN52 makes a bare `local += elem` ambiguous whatever its type), so it belongs to
// `1210b-a-nullable-source-into-a-local-is-still-refused.loft` rather than to this file.
//
// The warning COUNT is an assertion in its own right: four cells warn, three must not, and
// `@EXPECT_WARNING` scores both directions — an extra warning fails the file exactly as a
// missing one does.
//
// @falsified-at: 54826d97 — interpret exit 101 -> 0, interpret panicked -> clean, native exit
//   1 -> 0. On the parent commit the first cell panics the interpreter and native does not
//   compile, so the guard does not run at all and the channel is the process exit. The keyed
//   cell needs its own reading: it COMPILES there and answers silently, so for that one the
//   channel is the warning count, not the exit.
//
// @EXPECT_WARNING: a nullable `vector<integer>?` is stored into the appended value of the non-null type `vector<integer>`
// @EXPECT_WARNING: a nullable `vector<integer>?` is stored into the appended value of the non-null type `vector<integer>`
// loft#1434 / loft#1449 — the keyed type is named as the SOURCE writes it (`hash<E1210[k]>`),
// not in the schema's debug spelling.  `name` and `source_name` are one body carrying a flag
// through its own recursion, so a wrapper cannot re-spell the payload it wraps.
// @EXPECT_WARNING: a nullable `vector<E1210>?` is stored into the appended value of the non-null type `hash<E1210[k]>`
//   ⚠ That control is on no public ref, so this receipt records a falsification that
//   cannot be re-run — see TESTING.md § The patch receipt for the form that can be.

struct E1210 { k: integer, nm: text }
struct D1210 { c: vector<integer> }
struct K1210 { h: hash<E1210[k]> }

fn main() {
  s1210: vector<integer>? = [22, 33];

  // ---- the filed shape: a nullable vector into a dense vector FIELD ----
  d1210 = D1210 { c: [11] };
  d1210.c += s1210;
  assert(len(d1210.c) == 3, "the store proceeds and concatenates");
  assert(d1210.c[0] == 11, "what the field already held is kept");
  assert(d1210.c[1] == 22, "the source's first element follows it");
  assert(d1210.c[2] == 33, "and its last element ends the vector");

  // ---- an ACTUALLY-null source: the store proceeds, and appends nothing ----
  z1210: vector<integer>? = null;
  dz1210 = D1210 { c: [11] };
  dz1210.c += z1210;
  assert(len(dz1210.c) == 1, "appending an absent vector appends nothing");
  assert(dz1210.c[0] == 11, "and leaves what the field held untouched");

  // ---- a nullable ELEMENT rather than a nullable collection ----
  // Written with BRACKETS, and the brackets are the point rather than a detail. @PLAN52 requires
  // them for a bare element whatever its type, and loft#1223 made the nullable spelling meet the
  // same rule — before that, `dn1210.c += n1210` was ACCEPTED here while the dense
  // `dn1210.c += 11` was refused, so the `?` spelling of one statement was more permissive than
  // the plain one. This cell moved to the bracketed form with its assertions unchanged, which is
  // what says loft#1223 changed the SPELLING and not the store: the element still lands, still
  // reads back null, and the collection cells above are untouched.
  //
  // ⚠ This cell no longer warns, and that is loft#1232 rather than a property of this file: a
  // nullable value inside a vector LITERAL is stored into a dense `vector<integer>` with no
  // diagnostic at all. The `@EXPECT_WARNING` list below is one shorter for that reason.
  n1210: integer? = null;
  dn1210 = D1210 { c: [11] };
  dn1210.c += [n1210];
  assert(len(dn1210.c) == 2, "a nullable element is appended as one element");
  assert(dn1210.c[0] == 11, "after what the field already held");
  assert(dn1210.c[1] == null, "and the null is representable, so it reads back null");

  // ---- the KEYED destination: the half that used to be silent ----
  ks1210: vector<E1210>? = [E1210 { k: 1, nm: "one" }, E1210 { k: 2, nm: "two" }];
  k1210 = K1210 { h: [] };
  k1210.h += ks1210;
  assert(len(k1210.h) == 2, "a keyed field takes every record the nullable vector holds");
  assert(k1210.h[2].nm == "two", "and places each one by its own key");

  // ---- CONTROL: the dense source, which must be correct and SILENT ----
  ds1210: vector<integer> = [44];
  dd1210 = D1210 { c: [11] };
  dd1210.c += ds1210;
  assert(len(dd1210.c) == 2, "a dense source concatenates as it always did");
  assert(dd1210.c[1] == 44, "with its element after the field's own");

  // ---- CONTROL: both discharged spellings, which the warning names as the cure ----
  dq1210 = D1210 { c: [11] };
  dq1210.c += s1210?;
  assert(len(dq1210.c) == 3, "`s?` appends what the undischarged spelling appends");
  assert(dq1210.c[2] == 33, "with the same last element");

  dc1210 = D1210 { c: [11] };
  dc1210.c += s1210 ?? [];
  assert(len(dc1210.c) == 3, "`s ?? []` appends what the undischarged spelling appends");
  assert(dc1210.c[2] == 33, "with the same last element");
}
